 rne/
SUMMARY:Young Readers' Author Event — Paper Trails by Amelia Thorne
DESCRIPTION:Author Amelia Thorne will be talking about her coming-of-age hi
 storical novel\, Paper Trails\, at the library at 10am on 5 December.\n\nP
 erfect for readers aged 12-18 and crossover YA fans | All welcome | Drop i
 n\nAbout the Book\nIt’s 1989 in London\, the Cold War between East and W
 est is finally coming to an end\, and thirteen-year-old Milly has a school
  project to find out about her family history. But all she knows is that h
 er grandad was a doorman at the Savoy – and that\, long ago\, he was sup
 posedly a Russian prince\, back when Russia still had princes. Could that 
 really be true?\nSo\, when she discovers an old diary written in Russian\,
  she enlists the help of her schoolfriend Ilya\, whose family has fled the
  Soviet Union\, to unravel Nicolai’s story.\nAs they piece together the 
 unbelievable details of his escape from the Red Army and the hardships\, l
 ove and kindness he encountered as he made his way across Europe\, Milly s
 ees an opportunity to heal the tragedies of the past – with unexpected c
 onsequences.\nAbout the Author\nAmelia Thorne has written for Think Equal\
 , an organisation working with schools globally to promote social and emot
 ional education\, and for Apple Press\, set up to provide material for chi
 ldren without access to technology during the pandemic. She was part of th
 e creative team behind Mr Glue Stories\, an interactive story app. She rec
 ently published a middle-grade novel\, A Leap of Hope\n\n
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.phcl.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/
 Amelia-Thorne.png
